On-premise systems are self-hosted, meaning the software and hardware are located and managed
within the user's own premises. Cloud systems, on the other hand, are hosted by a third-party
provider and accessed remotely over the internet. Think of a restaurant using on-premise
software: their point-of-sale (POS) system is installed directly on their own computers,
while a cloud-based POS system would be accessed from any device with an internet connection.

On-Premise Systems:
--------------------
Location:
Software and hardware are physically located within the organization's premises.

Control:
The organization has full control over its infrastructure, including hardware, software, and data.

Maintenance:
The organization is responsible for all maintenance, updates, and upgrades of the system.

Security:
The organization is responsible for securing its own infrastructure and data.

Scalability:
Scalability can be limited by the organization's hardware capabilities.

Cost:
May have higher upfront costs for hardware and infrastructure, but long-term costs can be predictable.

Cloud Systems:
--------------
Location:
Software and hardware are hosted by a third-party provider in their data centers, accessed
remotely over the internet.

Control:
The organization typically has less control over the underlying infrastructure, but more control
over the software and data.

Maintenance:
The provider handles all maintenance, updates, and upgrades.

Security:
The provider is responsible for the security of the infrastructure, but the organization
is responsible for securing its data.

Scalability:
Highly scalable, allowing the organization to adjust resources as needed.

Cost:
Typically lower upfront costs, with recurring subscription fees.